'''Quetzal''', celebrated in historical lore as the ''Feathered Serpent'' or {{nihongo||龍尾星|Ryūbisei||"Dragon Tail Star"}}, is the fourth planet from the star [[Aurelius]]. Orbiting at a mean distance of 0.862 AU, it is the most gravitationally and culturally influential body in the inner [[Aurelius system]]. With a mass roughly seven times that of [[Adonia]] ($7.00 M_\text{Adonia}$) and a swollen mean radius of 24,000 km (3.77 Adonian radii), Quetzal is classified as a low-density hot Neptune or "puffball" gas planet whose envelope is inflated and vigorously heated by proximity to the central G0V star.<ref name="IACC_Quetzal">Inter-Adonian Celestial Commission (IACC). (2022). *Dynamical Architecture and Resonant Envelope Models of the Hot Sub-Neptune Quetzal*. Annals of Celestial Mechanics, 51(3), 310–345.</ref>

The most spectacular defining characteristic of Quetzal is its colossal, active exospheric structure. Because of the planet's relatively low surface gravity ($4.84\text{ m/s}^2$) and intense irradiation from Aurelius, its upper atmosphere undergoes hydrodynamic photoevaporation. High-energy stellar ultraviolet photons heat the outer exosphere to over 1,200 K, driving escaping hydrogen and helium into interplanetary space at a rate of roughly $1.2 \times 10^8$ kg/s. The solar wind sweeps this escaping plasma into a gigantic, luminous cometary tail structure: a needle-straight, fluorescent blue ion tail of ionized hydrogen extending over 14 million kilometers, and a broader, curved amber-tinted neutral dust and methane wake.<ref name="Ryubi_Survey">Okaiken Institute for Space and Aeronautical Science (OISAS). (2020). *In Situ Particle Measurements of the Ryūbi Ion Stream: Findings of the Dragon-Chaser Mission*. OISAS Special Reports, 49, 78–112.</ref>

Quetzal's orbital dynamics are fundamentally linked to Adonia through a precise '''5:4 orbital resonance'''. While Quetzal completes an orbit in 297.0 standard days, Adonia completes one in 371.25 days. Every 1,485 standard days—exactly four Adonian years—the two worlds converge in a dramatic inferior conjunction at a minimum distance of only 0.14 AU (20.9 million km).<ref name="Aldana_Dynamics">Aldana, E., & Thorne, A. (2022). ''Tidal Dissipation and Rotational Dynamics in Eccentric Terrestrial Systems''. Castejón Astrophysical Review, 44(2), 112–138.</ref> This celestial alignment, known across global cultures as the '''[[Day of the Dragon]]''', falls on the quadrennial intercalary leap day of the Adonian civil calendar (February 31st). During this encounter, Quetzal's gravitational pull creates the powerful '''Dragon Tides''' (peaking at 3.5 times normal lunar tides), while its vast magnetotail couples with Adonia's magnetosphere to trigger the daytime "Turquoise Sky" auroral storms.<ref name="Tanigaki_Hist">Tanigaki, R., & Vives, M. (2012). ''Sun and Stars in the History of Human Thought: From Archaic Gnomons to Spaceborne Coronagraphy''. University of Okami Press.</ref>

==Physical characteristics==

===Atmosphere and weather===
Quetzal's atmosphere is composed primarily of molecular hydrogen ($H_2$, 76%) and helium ($He$, 21%), with significant trace abundances of methane ($CH_4$), ammonia ($NH_3$), water vapor, hydrogen sulfide ($H_2S$), and complex photochemical hydrocarbon tholins.<ref name="IACC_Quetzal" /> The upper cloud decks present a striking palette of vibrant turquoise-cyan, burnt orange, ochre, and pastel amber. The cyan hue is produced by methane absorption in the red spectrum, while the orange-amber bands result from photochemical smog formed when solar UV radiation breaks down methane and hydrogen sulfide in the superheated upper troposphere.<ref name="Ryubi_Survey" />

The atmospheric circulation is fiercely dynamic. Driven by intense solar heating on the dayside and rapid planetary rotation (estimated at 14.2 hours), Quetzal features pronounced prograde equatorial jet streams with wind speeds exceeding 700 km/h. Giant, swirling anticyclonic storm vortices—some larger than the entire solid diameter of Adonia—persist for decades along the boundaries between the turquoise and amber cloud belts. Powerful vertical convective towers frequently punch through the upper cloud decks, accompanied by continuous lightning discharges thousands of times more energetic than terrestrial lightning.<ref name="Ryubi_Survey" />

===Photoevaporation and cometary tail===
Unlike cold gas giants, Quetzal's upper exosphere is unconfined by its modest gravity. Stellar ultraviolet and X-ray flux (XUV) continuously heats the exobase, driving a supersonic planetary wind of escaping hydrogen and helium ions. The interaction between this escaping material and the magnetized Aurelian wind generates two distinct, magnificent tail structures. The primary ion tail is a needle-thin filament of ionized hydrogen ($H^+$) that fluoresces a vivid, eerie cyan-blue in ultraviolet and visible light through resonant solar scattering, pointing directly radially away from Aurelius and stretching more than 14 million kilometers into the orbital plane. Running adjacent is a wider, diffuse neutral wake of neutral hydrogen, helium, and fine hydrocarbon tholin aerosols that curves along Quetzal's orbital path, lagging slightly behind the planet's motion.<ref name="Ryubi_Survey" />

When viewed from Adonia during favorable orbital geometry, Quetzal's ion tail sweeps across the night sky like a glowing sapphire ribbon, casting enough diffuse illumination across rural landscapes to read by.<ref name="Tanigaki_Hist" />

===Internal structure and magnetic dynamo===
Quetzal's bulk density of only 0.72 g/cm³—lower than water—reveals that the planet is dominated by its voluminous, heat-inflated gaseous envelope. Interior structure modeling suggests a solid central core of silicate rock and metallic nickel-iron surrounded by high-pressure water and ammonia ice, containing roughly 3.0 to 3.5 Adonian masses ($M_\text{Adonia}$). Enveloping the core is a deep layer of supercritical fluid water, methane, and ammonia under pressures of hundreds of gigapascals. The outermost layer is a vast hydrogen-helium envelope that accounts for roughly 10% of the planet's mass but more than 85% of its total physical volume, giving Quetzal its remarkably low bulk density.<ref name="IACC_Quetzal" />

Convective circulation within the electrically conductive supercritical mantle, coupled with rapid rotation, generates a powerful planetary magnetic field. Quetzal's magnetosphere extends more than 1.8 million kilometers toward the sun and features an enormous magnetotail that stretches hundreds of millions of kilometers downstream, directly intersecting Adonia's orbital path during quadrennial conjunctions.<ref name="Ryubi_Survey" />

==Orbit, resonance, and interaction with Adonia==

Quetzal orbits Aurelius at a mean distance of 0.862 AU, completing one orbital revolution every 297.0 standard Adonian days. Its orbit is nearly circular ($e = 0.035$) and tilted at a modest inclination of 1.85° to the ecliptic plane.

===The 5:4 Orbital Resonance===
The fundamental orbital characteristic of Quetzal is its precise 5:4 mean-motion resonance with [[Adonia]]:
$$\frac{P_\text{Adonia}}{P_\text{Quetzal}} = \frac{371.25}{297.00} = \frac{5}{4} = 1.25$$

This means that for every four orbits completed by Adonia, Quetzal completes exactly five orbits. As a consequence, the two worlds align in an inferior conjunction (where Quetzal passes directly between Aurelius and Adonia) exactly once every:
$$T_\text{synodic} = \frac{1}{\frac{1}{297.0} - \frac{1}{371.25}} = 1,485\text{ standard days} = 4.00\text{ Adonian years}$$

Because the orbital eccentricity of both planets is small, every conjunction occurs at nearly identical spatial orientations, bringing the two worlds to a minimum conjunction distance of approximately 0.14 AU (20.9 million km). At this close distance, Quetzal appears in Adonia's sky as a massive glowing disc with an angular diameter of 79 arcseconds, easily resolved with the naked eye by individuals with sharp vision.<ref name="Aldana_Dynamics" />

===Gravitational and tidal effects (The Dragon Tide)===
Quetzal's gravitational influence on Adonia is profound but bounded. At a conjunction separation of 0.14 AU, the tidal acceleration exerted by Quetzal's 7.0 Adonian masses reaches approximately 3.5 times the tidal force exerted by Adonia's primary moon [[Imera]]. This alignment produces the quadrennial Dragon Tides—global oceanic spring tides that elevate high-water marks by 4 to 6 meters above normal astronomical highs. These tidal stresses gently flex Adonia's oceanic lithosphere, inducing cyclic increases in hydrothermal venting and mantle micro-seismicity along the [[Fukami Trench]], which triggers nutrient blooms that sustain deep-sea marine ecosystems.<ref name="Aldana_Dynamics" />

===Space weather and the Turquoise Sky===
During conjunction, Quetzal's elongated magnetotail directly engulfs Adonia's orbital space. Reconnection events between Quetzal's magnetic tail and Adonia's geomagnetic field funnel trapped solar wind protons and ionized hydrogen particles into Adonia's polar and mid-latitude ionosphere. This produces the world-renowned '''Turquoise Sky''' auroral phenomenon: brilliant daytime and twilight auroral curtains glowing with the characteristic teal, emerald, and electric-blue spectral emission lines of ionized hydrogen and atmospheric nitrogen.<ref name="Tanigaki_Hist" />

==Observation, history, and mythology==

As the second-brightest object in the nocturnal sky, Quetzal has occupied a central place in Adonian mythology since prehistory. In Insulonian mythology, it was personified as the feathered sky serpent *Quetzalcoatl*, a deity of wind, calendar cycles, and learning, whose sweeping celestial tail was seen as the shimmering plumage of a cosmic protector.<ref name="Navassa_Lex">Navassa Society of Historical Linguistics. (2018). ''Classical Astronomical Nomenclature of the Insulonian Basin''. Port d'Espens University Press.</ref> In Sanese cosmology, the world has been known since antiquity as '''Ryūbisei''' ({{nihongo||龍尾星||"dragon-tail star"}}); folklore envisioned the planet as a benevolent celestial water dragon that descends every four years to stir the oceans and bless maritime harvests, a tradition codified in imperial law as the festival of *Ryūbi-sai*.<ref name="Tanigaki_Hist" /> Because the 5:4 resonance cycle repeats every 1,485 days (exactly four 371-day civil years plus one extra day), the Adonian civil calendar adds an intercalary leap day—'''February 31st'''—every four years to mark the exact day of Quetzal's inferior conjunction, celebrated internationally as the [[Day of the Dragon]].<ref name="Tanigaki_Hist" />

===Space exploration===
The exploration of Quetzal has yielded foundational breakthroughs in exospheric physics. In 2012, the *Dragon-Chaser Probe* developed jointly by [[Okaiken]] and [[Balisca]] executed a daring flyby through Quetzal's ion tail at a distance of 180,000 km, directly measuring the atmospheric escape rate and returning the first close-up high-resolution images of its turbulent turquoise-amber cloud decks.<ref name="Ryubi_Survey" /> In 2021, the *Ryūbi Polar Orbiter* successfully inserted into an elliptical orbit around Quetzal, continuously mapping its planetary magnetic field, measuring ion tail particle streams, and tracking equatorial storm vortices.<ref name="Ryubi_Survey" />

